{ignore}
<p><code>{extends 'layout'}</code> makes a page use a layout as its frame. It is not a tag
that runs while the page renders: it is resolved in the text while the page is assembled,
after the PHP and before anything renders, and taken out. The layout then replaces the frame
the page would otherwise get - the <code>_inits.pad</code> and <code>_exits.pad</code> of its
directories, and the <code>_common</code> wrapper.</p>

<p>The layout is a template named from the application root, like a
<a href="?tag/page"><code>{page}</code></a>; keeping layouts in a directory that starts with
an underscore (<code>_layouts/</code>) keeps them from being pages of their own. What the page
holds outside its <a href="?tag/block"><code>{block}</code></a> pairs goes where the layout
writes <code>&#64;page&#64;</code> - in front of the layout when it has none. The page's top-level
blocks override the layout's blocks of the same name, and
<a href="?tag/parent"><code>{parent}</code></a> inside one of them brings back the content it
overrides. A layout may extend a layout of its own; the chain is followed up to the
outermost.</p>

<p>The tag must stand directly in the page's own template, outside any other tag; one found
anywhere else - in a snippet, a custom tag, inside an <code>{if}</code> - is an error under
the strict check. Under the strict check a block of an extending page that overrides nothing
is reported as well. <code>{meta layout='...'}</code> does the same as
<code>{extends}</code>.</p>

<p>Because the frame is replaced, a page that extends a layout and is opened on its own shows
the layout and nothing of the application's wrapper. Inside a <code>{page}</code>, as in the
examples here, the included page is assembled the same way and brings its layout along.</p>
{/ignore}
